


                              Introduction to Programming 1
          
          
                               BEGINNING DATA BASE DESIGN 
          
                               Loading time:  1-2 minutes
           
          
          
                                 INTRODUCTION & HOOKUP
          
          Now that you have your Personal Computer and know basically
          how to operate it, the next step is learning how to put its
          power to work for you.  This Programming Kit opens the world
          of programming to you and introduces you to the concepts of
          DATA BASE DESIGN.
          
          By DATA BASE DESIGN, we mean putting together information in
          a way that you can use later.  This program takes you
          through the actual design of a data base system for your
          Commodore 64. The program can store, retrieve and print out
          anything from mailing lists to a date reminder.
          
          There are two versions of DATA BASE DESIGN included as part
          of this program package:
          
                  Version 1 - Basic Program             "D/B1"
          
                  Version 2 - Enhanced Program          "D/B2"
          
          
          We will cover multi-dimension string arrays, FOR/NEXT loops,
          subroutines, menus, special input routines, disk storage,
          sorting techniques, and more. We will also cover our 8-step
          approach to program design and how to apply these steps to
          future program designs.
          
          
          Before you begin, you may want to go through your Commodore
          64 User Guide just for review. When you're ready to start,
          hook up your computer, including your disk drive and
          printer.
          
          
          HOW TO USE THE DATA BASE SYSTEM
          
          Initializing your data disk
          
          Before a menu appears on your screen, you will be asked "Do
          You Want To Initialize The System? (Y/N)". Because you are
          starting with no data stored on a blank disk, you must type
          "Y."  Later, when you have data stored on the disk, type "N."
          
          If you typed "Y", the computer will ask if you are sure this
          is what you want. Type "Y", if you're sure, "N" if you're
          not.
          
          


                                       1





          Later, when you type "N", you will be instructed to insert
          the disk which holds your own data.  When you type -C-, the
          computer will start reading your data from the disk.
          
          Now it starts to get exciting--here comes our menu!
 
         
           VIEW/MODIFY INFORMATION
    
          Press l, VIEW/MODIFY INFORMATION.  Then press the RETURN
          key.
          
          You will view a list of general information.
          
          1.  To fill in the blanks, Press -Y- in response to
          CHANGES?(Y/N).  Enter l in response to WHICH ITEM? Now,
          enter the type of data you will be storing, such as
          addresses, recipes, and so on (30 characters maximum). Then
          press RETURN.
          
          2.  Press -Y- again, then enter 2 in response to WHICH ITEM?
          Enter any comments you wish.  Then press RETURN.
          
          3.  Press -Y- then enter 3 in response to WHICH ITEM?  The
          last date you used this system should be entered--for
          example, today's date.  Enter today's date, then press
          RETURN. Now press -N- in response to CHANGES? (Y/N); then,
          press -M- to return to the menu.
          
          
          ENTERING ITEMS
          
          When the menu is displayed on the screen, press 2 to ENTER
          NEW ITEMS.  You will see the item number and line number.
          The system automatically starts this section with the next
          ITEM to be filled in with data.  Example:  if you previously
          entered 20 ITEMS, ITEM 21 will be first when entering this
          section. If you make any errors, don't worry. You can easily
          correct them later.
          
          1.  At the bottom you will see INPUT LINE 1?  Type line 1 of
          your item, then press RETURN.
          
          
          2.  Next, at the bottom, you will see INPUT LINE 2? Enter
          line 2, then press RETURN.
          
          3.  Continue entering lines as requested by the system.
          
          NOTE: Be sure to answer every question in the general
          information display. Additionally, as you enter your data,
          each line of each record must have at least one character on
          it. If you have no information to enter on a line, type an N
          (for none) on that line. A STRING TOO LONG situation may
          result if you do not fill in all blank lines.
          
          
          
          

                                       2





              EXAMPLE: CORRECT               INCORRECT
          
            123) l John Doe             123) l John Doe
                 2 l00 Main St.              2 l00 Main St.
                 3 N                         3
          
          When you see CHANGES?(Y/N) at the bottom, you have entered a
          complete record. Look it over.  If there are any errors,
          press -Y- in response to CHANGES?(Y/N). Enter the line
          number to be changed, then enter the correction.  Press
          RETURN.
          
          If everything is correct, press -N- in response to
          CHANGES?(Y/N).  The next item number will appear.
          When you want to return to the Menu, press RETURN without
          entering any data in response to LINE 1?
    
          
          VIEW/MODIFY BY ITEM NUMBER
          
          When the menu is displayed on the screen, press menu item 3
          to VIEW/MODIFY BY ITEM NUMBER.  Input the item number you
          would like to view/modify.  It will be listed, followed by a
          number of subsequent items.
          
          To make changes, press -Y- in response to CHANGES(Y/N).
          Enter the record number, then the line number. Then, enter
          the replacement line.  Press RETURN.
          
          Next, you will see Next Page or Menu?  Press -P- to view the
          next page of items or-M- to return to the Menu.
          
          
          VIEW/MODIFY BY ITEM ELEMENT (ENHANCED VERSION)
          
          When the menu is displayed on the screen, press 4 to
          VIEW/MODIFY BY ITEM ELEMENT.  When the computer asks you to
          input an item or name, you can enter up to 30 characters.
          When requested, input the line you want the computer to
          search.  This option can be used to search by full words or
          word fragments (numbers too).  Say you would like to
          retrieve all of your addresses in Chicago.  Enter CHICAGO or
          any word fragment such as CHI. (Longer search words result
          in faster searches.)
          
          
          NOTE: As the computer searches through records, the number
          of the record currently being searched is displayed at the
          bottom of your screen.
          
          After the computer ends the search, you will see LIST?(Y/N).
          Press -Y- to see a list, or -N- if you don't.
          
          After the listing of each group of items, you will see
          CHANGES? (Y/N).  Press -Y- to make changes to a record or
          -N- for no changes.  If you press -Y- the computer will ask
          you to enter the record number, then the line number.  Next,
          enter the new line the way you want it to appear.  Press RETURN.
          

                                       3





          After pressing -N- for CHANGES? (Y/N), you will see either
          PRESS-M-FOR MENU or PRESS -C- TO CONTINUE.  Pressing -M-
          will return you to the Menu.  If the PRESS -C- instruction
          appears, this means the computer has retrieved more records
          that it can display on a single screen. Pressing -C- will
          list the next page of records.
          
          X-SEARCH OR "CROSS SEARCH" (ENHANCED VERSION)
          
          When you return to the Menu after doing a search by ITEM
          ELEMENT, you will see X-SEARCH? (Y/N).  Press -Y- to do a
          cross search, or -N- if you don't want to cross search.
          
          What does X-SEARCH do?  X-SEARCH, also called CROSS SEARCH,
          is a tool for narrowing down your search for records.  It
          allows you to repeat a search through records you have just
          retrieved from your entire list.
          
          EXAMPLE: Let's say you have 80 addresses in the system.  To
          pick out all the people you know in Chicago, use Menu Item
          4, VIEW/MODIFY BY ITEM ELEMENT. Enter CHICAGO for ITEM NAME,
          then enter the line the city should be in (usually line 3).
    
          You will see the number of matches the computer has found
          listed at the bottom (say 30).  Now to search this list of
          30 addresses further, press -N- in response to LIST?(Y/N).
          
          NOTE: Press -Y- if you would like to view the list.
          
          Press -Y- again in response to X-SEARCH?(Y/N).
          
          Now, suppose you would like to view only your Chicago
          friends who live on Western Avenue use Menu Item 4 again,
          type in "Western"; then, press RETURN. When the computer
          asks, enter the line number your street name is in.  Now,
          the computer will search your list of Chicago addresses for
          your Chicago friends living on Western Avenue.  After the
          search is over, LIST?Y/N) will appear.  Press -Y- if you
          want the list and -N- if you don't.
          
          You can X-SEARCH a list as many times as you want,
          narrowing your list down to the specific records
          you want to view.
          
          
          PRINTING (ENHANCED VERSION)
          
          If you want a printed copy of your records, use Option 5 to
          transfer or to "dump" your records to the printer.
          
          In response to the warning: "Is your printer connected?"
          check your printer connection, press -Y- if it is connected
          and -N- if it is not.
          
          Next, in response to ALL AMOUNTS OR LAST SORT?(A/L), select
          which group of amounts you want to print;
          
                  All Amounts:  Print all the records in your Data
                                Set.
          
                                       4

  
  


                   Last Sort:  Print only the records found in the last
                                search you made using Menu Item 4.
          
          If you want to stop in the middle of the print routine,
          press the [F1] key. The printing stops after the computer
          completes the record currently being printed.
          
          NOTE: Keep the [F1] key pressed until the printing stops.
          
          
          STORING DATA
          
          When you are finished with your data base design and want to
          save it to disk, press menu item 4 on the basic program or 6
          on the enhanced program.
          
          The screen prompt reads "Do You Want To Save Your Data On
          DISK?(Y/N)".  Type Y to save your data, and N if you don't.
          If you type N, the program ends.
          
          The screen prompt reads "Insert Data Disk Into the Disk
          Drive ...Then Press -C- To Continue."  Put your data disk
          into the disk drive, close the protective gate; then press
          C.
          
          The computer now starts writing your data onto your data
          disk.  The menu item being saved is shown on the screen.
          Make sure your disk has been formatted. Your disk drive
          manual may refer to formatting as "newing a disk.|" 
         
          These terms are interchangable for the process of
          setting up a blank disk for your use.
          
          After the computer stores your data, the program ends.
          
          NOTE: D/B1 and D/B2 can each contain 100 items with 8 lines
          per item. Later, when you write the DATA BASE DESIGN for
          yourself, you'll be able to pick the number of items and
          lines per item.
          
          
          BEFORE WE BEGIN PROGRAMMING. . .
          
          This Programmming Kit is made up of the following BASIC
          commands and functions.
          
          
                   ABS               IF                PRINT
                   CHR$              INPUT             PRINT#
                   CLR               INPUT #           REM
                   CLOSE             LEN               SPC
                   DIM               LEFT$             STR$
                   FOR               NEXT              TAB
                   GET               ON                TI
                   GOSUB             OPEN              VAL
                   GOTO              POKE
          
          Your most important resource while going through this manual
          is your Commodore 64 or 128 Users Manual.

                                       5





          If you ever have a question on a "Basic" command,
          check the index of your Users Manual.
          
          Your manual tells you what each basic command does and how
          it executes it. This programming kit, on the other hand,
          shows you how to put these commands together into a working
          program.
          
          
          NOTE: We suggest you purchase the "C-64 Programming
          Reference Guide" published by SAMS at your local book
          store.
          
          We will be using a "Top Down" design approach for our Data
          Base Design program. By "Top Down" we mean we'll start with
          the general ideas and objectives of our program and work
          down to the specific basic commands to accomplish what we
          want to do.  There are eight steps to our method of "Top
          Down" program design:
          
                1.  General Concept
                2.  Displays
                3.  Flow Chart
                4.  Algorithms
                5.  Writing the Program
                6.  Typing in the Program
                7.  Debugging the Program
                8.  Modifying the Program
          
          Each of these steps will be discussed in detail as we go
          through DATA BASE DESIGN.
          
          
          VERSION 1:  BASIC PROGRAM
          
          General Concept

          A Program is the set of instructions that tells the computer
          what to do. For this step, we write out on paper what we would
          like our program to do (sometimes this covers many pages).
          
                  1.  We want to set up our own data base format.
          
                  2.  We want a menu-driven program.
          
                  3.  We want to enter our data into the system.
          
                  4.  We want to view and/or modify the stored data.
          
                  5.  We want to keep track of the last revision date
                      and any general information.
          
                  6.  We want our Data Base Design to store data on
                      disk or cassette (and retrieve it, too!)
          
          Displays
          
          At last!  Now it's time to design the displays for your Data
          Base Design!  Draw a square on a piece of graph paper. Let's
          go over some items we want to include:
                                       6





                  1.  Menu Display
          
                      a)  A title for the menu.
          
                      b)  Menu items properly spaced and easy to
                          read.
          
                      c)  Directions or prompts in a common
                          location.
          
                  2.  View Data Display
          
                      a)  Requested data in the upper 4/5ths of the
                          display.
          
                      b)  Directions or prompts in a common location
                          (the lower 1/5 of the display).
          
          Let's set up our displays with the approximate print
          locations on our drawing. Don't be afraid to use your
          eraser.  You want to produce a drawing that looks good and
          works well.
          
                             [SEE FIGURES 1 & 2]
          
          You can draw the Data Storing/Retrieving display by copying
          the display from the DATA BASE DESIGN programs we have
          provided as a sample.
          
          
          Flow Charts
          
          We must now decide on the general flow of our program...what
          will happen and when.  A flow chart quickly and easily lets
          you visualize program flow.  It is a map of the program you
          are about to create.
          
          First, you need to plan what specific tasks our main program
          must do to manipulate data.
          
                These tasks are:
   
                  1.  Initialize the screen.
          
                  2.  Define our variables.
          
                  3.  Define our data base parameters.
          
                  4.  Initialize the data base (if needed).
          
                  5.  Read in data base information from disk.
          
                  6.  Create and print a menu.
          
                  7.  Write the instructions that make the menu work.
          
                  8.  Enter new items.
          
                  9.  View/Modify by Item Number.
          
                                       7





                 10.  View/Modify General Information.
          
                 11.  Store our data on disk.
          
                 12.  End the program.
          
          That's it for now.  Don't be worried about twelve tasks. When
          we're finished, twelve tasks will be easy for you.
          
          Next, we must draw the flow chart. The Flow Chart
          illustration is shown in Figure 3.
          
                                [SEE FIGURE 3]
          
          Algorithms
          
          What is an algorithm (pronounced Al-go-rhythm)?  It's a
          sequence of mathematical or logical steps that calculate or
          execute a given task.  We will use BASIC commands to make
          our computer perform the tasks we have outlined in the
          algorithm.
          
          You have already decided  our main algorithm by the flow
          charts made in the previous section.  All we do now is break
          each task in the main algorithm into a simple algorithm of
          its own.
          
                   1.  Initializing the screen
          
                       a)  Clear the display.
          
                       b)  Change to the upper/lower case mode.
          
                       c)  Disable the SHIFT/COMMODORE KEY combination.
          
                       d)  Define display color and print colors.
          
                   2.  Defining the variables
          
                       a)  Define all the variables we will use for
                           the cursor and general information.
          
                   3.  Defining data base parameters
          
                       a)  Define the size of the data array we need.
          
                       b)  Define the number of lines per item.

                       c)  Define the maximum number of items.
          
                       d)  Define the maximum number of lines.
          
                       e)  Define the starting record number.
          
                       f)  Define the number of records displayed on
                           the screen.
          



                                       8





                   4.  Initializing the data base
          
                       a)  Set the current item to be read to be the
                           first item.
          
                   5.  Reading the data base information
          
                       a)  Open your data file.
          
                       b)  Read in the current item to be read, lines
                           per item, items per screen, maximum records
                           that can be read and the general information.
          
                       c)  Read in the data base items and lines.
          
                       d)  Print the current record being read.
          
                       e)  Close the file.
          
                   6.  Printing our menu on the screen
          
                   7.  Menu Driver
          
                       a)  Receive the user's response.
          
                       b)  Use an ON command to take us to the menu
                           item we want the computer to perform.
          
                   8.  Entering new items
          
                       a)  Determine the next item to be read.
          
                       b)  Receive input from the first line.
          
                       c)  Branch back to the menu if the input for
                           line one is" "(Null, no input).
          
                       d)  Receive input for the remaining lines.
          
                       e)  Make changes if necessary.
          
                       f)  Branch back to 8a.
          
                   9.  View/Modify by item number
          
                       a)  Determine the beginning item number.
          
                       b)  Display a defined number of items on the
                           screen (we decided on that number in
                           algorithm 3).
          
                       c)  Make changes if necessary.
          
                       d)  Determine whether more items exist after
                           those listed on the display.  If more items
                           exist, branch to algorithm 6.
 
 


                                       9


 


                       e)  Determine whether the user wishes to view
                           more items (branch back to 9b) or view the
                           menu (branch to algorithm 6).
          
                 10.   View/modify general information
          
                       a)  Print out the general information.
          
                       b)  Determine whether changes need to be made.
          
                       c)  If no changes are needed, branch to the
                           menu (algorithm 6).
          
                       d)  Make the changes and branch back to 10a.)
          
                 11.   Storing data
          
                       a)  Determine if data is to be stored.
          
                       b)  If data is not to be stored, branch to
                           algorithm 12.
          
                       c)  Open your data file.
          
                       d)  Store the next item to be read, lines per
                           item, items per screen and the general
                           information.
          
                       e)  Write the data base items and lines into
                           our data file.
          
                       f)  Print the current record being stored.
          
                       g)  Close the file.
          
                       h)  Branch to algorithm 12.
          
                 12.   Ending the run
          
                       a)  Print an END RUN  statement.
          
                       b)  Branch into an infinite loop.
          
          Writing the Program
          
          Now we're ready to write out our program on paper. First, we
          must decide on the variables you will need.  Let's look
          through our algorithms, choose some variables and compare
          them with those that follow below:
          
              ap       -numerical value of menu choice
          
              c$       -GET command input string
          
              d        -input number variable
          
              dd       -input number variable
          


                                       10





              dd$ (100,8)  -data storage array
          
              e$       -erasing character string
          
              f        -current item to be listed
          
              g        -for next loop counter

              i        -for next loop counter
          
              j        -for next loop counter
          
              k        -current item being viewed
          
              l        -number of lines per item
          
              lr       -largest numbers of items
          
              m        -number of items on the screen at one time
          
              mp       -screen clearing flag
          
              pp       -input number variable
          
              p$       -cursor control variable
          
              pi$      -cursor control variable
          
              ri$      -revision control variable
          
              s$       -cursor control variable
          
              tp       -jiffy clock working variable
          
              t$       -input string variable
          
              t% (101)    -sort/search array
          
              x        -next item to be added
          
              xx       -input number variable
          
              yi$      -general information comment variable
          
              zi$      -general information subject variable
          
          How did you do?  Even though their logic may be the same,
          different programmers usually come up with somewhat
          different variables.  We did use "%" after one array because
          we are using integers (no values in that array will contain
          decimals) and "%" will save storage space.  See your
          Commodore Users Guide for more on this.
          
          We're now ready to write our basic Data Base Design program.
          Study each explanation line together with the corresponding
          program listing line.  Look at each basic command and each
          function in that program listing line before moving on to
          the next line.  Make sure you understand why the command or
          function is in that line and what it does.

                                       11





          Don't be afraid to turn to your Users Guide if a
          question still remains.  Take your time, and you'll
          get this program easily.
          
          
          PROGRAM EXPLANATION LINES - BASIC VERSION
          
            1.  INITIALIZE THE SCREEN
          
          
              LINE NO.        ACTION THE COMPUTER TAKES
          
                 100          Labels Programming Kit
          
                 110          Labels Data Base Design

                 120          Clears screen, switches to lower
                              and disables the SHIFT/COMMODOR KEY
                              combination.
          
                 130          Clears any old variables, changes
                              border and display to black, and
                              characters to white.
          
          2.  DEFINING THE VARIABLES
          
                 150-160      Defines cursor positioning array
          
                 180          Defines a blank string to write
                              over any messages that might have been
                              previously written.
          
                 190          Initializes the general information
                              variables
          
          3.  DEFINE THE DATA BASE VARIABLES
          
                140          Defines the dimension of your data
                             holding array and our search array
          
                460          Sets lines per item to be 8.  Sets items
                             per screen display at 2.  Sets the item
                             limit to be 100.  Sets x (the next item
                             number to be read) to be 1.
          
                                                Maximum items
                              Lines per          per System
                                Item                Load
          
                                 1                   800
                                 2                   400
                                 3                   266
                                 4                   200
                                 5                   160
                                 6                   133
                                 7                   114
                                 8                   100
          


                                       12





          NOTE: If you wish to change the number of lines per item
          after you get both the basic and enhanced programs running,
          change the variable in line 460 to the following values:
          
          1= number        m= items       ir=next        x=next item
          of lines           per          number of      number to
          per item         display        records        be read
          
          
          Number of    1 = 8        m = 2        ir = 100     x = 1 
          Lines       should be    should be     should be    should be
          
            1           1 = 1        m = 10      ir = 800       x = 1
            2           1 = 2        m = 6       ir = 400       x = 1
            3           1 = 3        m = 5       ir = 260       x = 1
            4           1 = 4        m = 4       ir = 200       x = 1
            5           1 = 5        m = 3       ir = 160       x = 1
            6           1 = 6        m = 3       ir = 130       x = 1
            7           1 = 7        m = 2       ir = 110       x = 1
            8           1 = 8        m = 2       ir = 100       x = 1
          
          ...and the variables in line 140 should be changed to the
          following values.

               Number of      d$(100.8)        1%(101)
               Lines per      should be        should be
                 item         changed to       changed to
          
                  1          d$(800,1)         t%(801)
                  2          d$(400,4)         t%(401)
                  3          d$(260,3)         t%(261)
                  4          d$(200,4)         t%(201)
                  5          d$(160,5)         t%(161)
                  6          d$(130,6)         t%(131)
                  7          d$(110,7)         t%(111)
                  8          d$(100,8)         t%(101)
          
          For example, if we wanted a mailing list four lines long,
          lines 140 and 460 would look like this:
          
                140        dimd$(200,4),t%(201)
          
                460        1 = 4:m = 4:Ir = 200: x = 1
          
          4.  INITIALIZE THE DATA BASE
          
             LINE NO.         ACTION THE  COMPUTER TAKES
          
                300           Labels the array initialization section
          
                310           Prints "YOUR DATA BASE DESIGN" centered
                              and eight lines from the top of the display
          
                320           Prints (Your Name) Data Base two lines
                              below the statement listed on line 310
          
                330           Prints "Intialize `the System?(Y/N)"
                              on display line 21
          

                                       13





                340           Clears out the keyboard buffer
          
                350           Receives a response from the user
          
                360           If the response is "Y", branches
                              to 390
          
                370           If the response is "N", branches
                              to 480
          
                380           If the response was neither "Y" or
                              "N", branches to 340 to get a correct
                              response
          
                390- 410      Erases any previous messages displayed
                              on display line 21.  The computer asks
                              the user if he is sure he wants to
                              start his records from the beginning.
          
                420           Receives a response from the user
          
                430           Erases the message that was printed
                              by lines 390-410
          
                440           If the response if "N", branches to
                              310
          
                450           If the response is anything but "Y",
                              branches to 390
          
                460           Sets lines per item to be 8.  Sets
                              items per screen at 2.  Sets the item
                              limit to be 100.  Initializes x (the
                              current number of items) to be 1
          
                470           Clears the screen and branch to
                              610
          
                480           Labels the data retrieval routine
          
                490           Clears the screen
           
          
          5.  READ DATA BASE INFORMATION
          
          
                LINE NO.      ACTION THE COMPUTER TAKES
          
                500-510       Prints "INSERTS THE DATA DISK"
                              and press -C- to continue on display
                              line 21
                520           Clears out the keyboard buffer
          
                530           Receives a response from the
                              user
          




                                       14





                540           Clears the screen and prints
                              "*** READING DATA FROM DISK ***"
          
                550           Opens the sequential file
                              "data" on disk
          
                560           Reads x,l,m,ir,zi$,yi$,ri$,
                              from the file
          
                590           Creates a nested loop (one loop
                              within another) to read all the
                              information from "data" file on disk.
                              Line 590 also prints the item number
                              currently being read
          
                600           Closes the file and clears the
                              display
          
          
          6.  PRINT YOUR MENU ON THE SCREEN
          
          
                610           Labels your "Data Base Design Menu"
                              section
          
                620           Sets your display clearing flag to
                              zero
          
                630-690       Clears the display and prints the
                              menu
          
          7.  MENU INSTRUCTIONS
          
                790           Erases any messages in display line
                              21
          
