          Typing the Program
          
          Now that you have the program figured out, enter the
          complete BASIC program listed in the program listing
          booklet.
          
          NOTE: After you have typed in the program, save it on your
          own disk. (Use SAVE "D/B2",8.) See your computer manual for
          details on saving.
          
          
          Debugging
          
          Most computer programs have bugs (problems) in them at
          first.  Debugging is what the programmer (that's you!) does
          to get those bugs out.
          
          Debugging usually involves reviewing your program in detail
          until you are satisfied with it.
          
          Now that you have your program safely stored on disk, it's
          time to try it out.  Type RUN, then press RETURN.
          
          If it works the first time, you're a programming whiz!  Very
          seldom does a computer program work perfectly the first time
          (or even the 10th time).
 
          To debug your program, use your computer's editing
          capabilities and make your program work.
          
          NOTE: See your computer manual on how to work the list,
          insert, delete, and cursor movement commands.
          
          If you get an error code, LIST the line in the code and
          correct any problems.
          
          NOTE: The error code explanation in your computer manual
          helps.
          
          Some bugs may not be obvious. The only way to find such
          errors is by testing the program while looking for anything
          that doesn't make sense.
          
                                       22





          Some common bugs are:
          
                  *Missing punctuation in print statements
          
                  *Missing lines
          
                  *Forgetting to type in a line from your written
                   version

          Again, take your time, read these and the Commodore
          instructions, be careful and have fun. You're well on your
          way to being a top notch programmer!
         
          Modifying the Program
          
          Now that we have a running program, it's time to do what
          most programmers do: modify our program. (That's why we try
          to leave space between line numbers). Here are some
          modifications you can make to your basic data base.
          
                 l.  You might like to view or modify your data by
                     item element.
          
                 2.  A cross-search option might be nice.
          
                 3.  Dump all your items to the printer, or maybe just
                     the items the computer searched for last.
          
                 4.  You may also want to do an input subroutine using
                     the GET command.  It's more versatile and you
                     don't have to worry about "redo" appearing on the
                     screen at any time.
          
          VERSION 11: ENHANCED PROGRAM
          
          General Concept
          
                  Believe it or not, you have already dealt with our
                  general concept.  In thinking of modifications for
                  the basic program, you have in effect, created the
                  concepts for the enhanced version.  So...on to Step 2!
          
          Displays
          
                  Your displays from the basic version are fine.
                  Because  of this, and the internal modifications in
                  your Data Base Design, we are leaving the displays
                  as they are.  If you wish to alter the displays
                  later, go right ahead.
  
          Flow Charts
          
                  Since there are no drastic changes in our program
                  other than the insertion of our four modifications,
                  we need only insert these modifications in the basic
                  version flow chart.  Figure 4 shows the flow chart
                  for the Enhanced Program.
          
                                    [SEE FIGURE 4]

                                       23
          




          NOTE:  For simplicity we have omitted the input routine from
          the flow chart.
          
          Algorithms
          
                  l.  View/Modify by Item Element
          
                      a)  Receive the name or word string the user
                          wishes to search for
          
                      b)  Receive the line number where this name or
                          word string is located
        
                      c)  Use a FOR/NEXT loop to move to each item,
                          comparing the name or word string with the
                          character string in the chosen line number.
                          If a match occurs, the item number is inserted
                          into the search array
          
                      d)  If the search array is empty (no match
                          occurs) the computer branches to the menu
          
                      e)  If one or more matches occur, determine
                          whether the items are to be listed
          
                      f)  If the matched items are to be listed,list
                          them, making any necessary changes
          
                      g)  Enable the X-SEARCH flag and branch to the menu
          
              2.  Cross-search
          
                      a)  If the cross-search flag is enabled,
                          determine if you want the X-SEARCH to
                          be performed
         
                      b)  If the cross-search is to be performed, only
                          items currently in the search array are
                          compared for matches (when the user searches
                          by item element)
          
                      c)  If the user chooses any other menu option,
                          the cross-search flag is disabled and all
                          searches are performed in the normal manner.
         
              3.  Dump records to the printer
          
                      a)  Make sure the printer is connected
          
                      b)  Determine whether all records, or last
                          sorted records are to be printed.
          
                      c)  Print a title from the general information
                          section
          
                      d)  Display the item currently being printed
          
                      e)  Print that item on the printer
          

                                       24





                      f)  Perform d) and e) above until the
                          computer prints all of the records you want
          
                      g)  Print the number of items that were printed
          
                      h)  Branch to the menu
      
              4.  Input Routine
          
                      a)  Place the cursor in the beginning location
          
                      b)  Set the line string (we'll call it t$) to
                          ""(the null string)
          
                      c)  Receive a character from the user
       
                      d)  If the character is the return key or the
                          length of our line string is larger than 29
                          characters, return to the main program
          
                      e)  If the character from the user is the
                          delete key and the length of t$ is larger
                          than 1, remove a character from t$ (our line
                          string) and print the new t$ string and
                          prompt
          
                      f)  If the received character is a character you
                          programmed the computer to accept, add the
                          character to start your line string and branch
                          back to c)
          
          You've now completed all the algorithms we set out to cover
          for the enhanced version.  Look them over to be sure.
          
          Writing the Program
          
          In your modification, you need to come up with more
          variables and arrays. Let's look through the last batch of
          algorithms and develop more variables to help program this
          modification.
          
                          cc$   =  holds "es" when printing "Matches"
          
                          ct    =  printed items counter
          
                          h$    =  input subroutine cursor
          
                          11    =  all/last sort print flag
          
                          ls    = length of search by item element
          
                          sl    =  line to be search when search by
                                   item element
          
                          na$   =  element to be searched for, when
                                   searching by item
          



                                       25





                          0     =  For-Next loop counter location
          
                          q     =  For-Next loop counter
          
                          s     =  X-search flag
          
               This Programming Kit comes complete with an enhanced
               program listing (take out your programming listing
               booklet) and a line-by-line explanation of that basic
               program.  Study each explanation line together with the
               corresponding program listing line.  Look at each basic
               command and function in that program listing line.
               Make sure you understand why the command or function is
               in that line and what it does before you go on to the
               next line.  Don't be afraid to turn to your Commodore
               User's Guide if a question still remains.
          
          
