                    1630         Calls the input subroutine. Determines
                                 the numerical value of t$.  Erases lines
                                 21 and 22 of the display.  If the
                                 numerical value of t$ is smaller than
                                 1 or larger than the current number of
                                 items, branches to 1620
          
                    1650         Prints "WHICH LINE NUMBER" on line 21
                                 of the display
          
                    1690         Calls the input subroutine. Sets d$
                                 to the value of t$. Erases lines 21
                                 and 22 of the display
          
                    2180         If c$ is equal to "1", calls the
                                 input subroutine and sets the comment
                                 variable to the input string t$
          
                    2190         If c$ is equal to "2", calls the
                                 input subroutine and sets the comment
                                 variable to the input string t$
          
                    2200         If c$ is equal to "3", calls the
                                 input subroutine and sets the revision
                                 date variable to the input string t$
          
          Typing the Program
          
          Now that you have the program figured out, turn on your
          computer (check ALL connections!) and type in the complete
          basic program as listed in the file labeled "Enhanced
          Program".
          
          Debugging
          
          Well, your enhanced program has now been written and typed.
          Now, go back and follow the debugging principles discussed
          earlier.  Once you're finished debugging the program, you'll
          have your own data base system.
          
          
          Modifying the Program
          
          We don't have any more modifications for you.  With a little
          effort, you can think of dozens of other improvements all on
          your own!
          
          
          Here are some more suggestions:
          
                  *Move a line of one item to the line of another
          
                  *Sort items alphabetically or numerically
          
                  *Store records on relative files
          

                                       31





                  *Perform calculations between values in item lines
          
                  *Produce variable line items
          
                  *Append lines to other items
          
                  *Merge items
          
                  *Split items
          
          NOTE: Searching and sorting are among the most common
          programming processes. Searching is a systematic examination
          of information in a given field of interest, like going
          through a list to find a key word.  Sorting is the process
          of arranging data into some order, like putting a list or
          file cards in alphabetical order. Mathematicians and
          computer scientists have spent thousands of research hours
          and have written thousands of books and papers on sorting
          and searching techniques.  Your local computer store, book
          store or public library has additional information.
          
          
          Odds and Ends
          
          While explaining the procedure for data base design, we have
          left in our program certain statements and commands that
          help show the processes being performed.  Some of these
          statements are no longer necessary and may be taken out.
          You can learn a lot by looking through your now-completed
          program and weeding out these statements and commands. We
          leave this as an exercise for you.
          
         
          What's next? We hope you'll take the ideas from this
          programming kit and apply them to programs of your own.
          When you have mastered this one, we recommend you try the
          other Melody Hall Programming kit: INTRODUCTION TO
          PROGRAMMING 2.  You'll learn even more about the art
          of programming.
          
          
          Troubleshooting
          
                System will not LOAD       *Check computer hookup
                                           *Try to load backup version
          
                System will not SAVE       *Check computer hookup
          
                System Glitch-Screen       *Reload program
                                            blanks out, program lost
                                            (sometimes due to power
                                            fluctuation)
          
                Bad SAVE-Loss of Data      *Reconstruct data from
                                            last good save
          
                Data will not load into    *Be sure you are using the
                the system                  correct data cassette or disk
          

                                       32




                Syntax Error               *Make sure lines are typed
                                            correctly
          
                                           *Check punctuation
          
                String too long            *Make sure all the lines of
                                            every item are not blank
          
                Device not present         *Printer not hooked on
                                            Drive not hooked on
          
                Bad Subscript              *Find an array with a
                                            number value larger than
                                            the specified range
          
                Device not present         *Turn on the required
                                            input/output devices
                                            disk or tape
          
                File not open              *The file specified
                                            must first be opened
          
                File open                  *The file requested is
                                            already open.  Close the
                                            file before reopening
          
                Illegal Quantity           *Find a number that is
                                            outside the allowable
          
                Next without For           *Find a NEXT statement
                                            that is out of place
          
                Out of Memory              *Find any misplaced
                                            GOSUP's or redefine
                                            smaller arrays
          
                Undef'd Statement          *Find a GOTO or
                                            GOSUB statement which
                                            attempts to define a line
                                            that doesn't exist    





















                                       33





     VERSION 1 - BASIC PROGRAM      "D/B1"
     
     100 REM"**** PROGRAMMING KIT 1 ****"
     110 REM"**** DATA BASE DESIGN ****"
     120 PRINT"[CLR]";CHR$(14);CHR$(8)
     130 CLR:POKE53280,0:POKE53281,0:PRINTCHR$(5)
     140 DIMD$(100,8),T%(101)
     150 P$="[HOME][21 DOWN]":P1$=P$+"[DOWN]"
     160 S$="[HOME][24 DOWN]"
     180 E$="[39 SPACES]"
     190 ZI$="INSERT DATA":RI$="INSERT DATE":YI$=ZI$
     300 REM"**** INITIALIZE DATAARRAY ****"
     310 PRINT"[CLR][8 DOWN]";TAB(6);"*** YOUR DATABASE DESIGN ***"
     320 PRINT"[DOWN]";TAB(10);"(C)1983 MELODY HALL CORP."
     330 PRINTP$;"INITIALIZE THE SYSTEM ?(Y/N)"
     340 GETC$:IFC$<>""THEN340
     350 GETC$:IFC$=""THEN350
     360 IFC$="Y"THENGOTO390
     370 IFC$="N"THENGOTO480
     380 GOTO340
     390 PRINTP$;E$:PRINTP$;"THIS WILL START YOUR DATA RECORDS"
     400 PRINT"FROM THE BEGINNING....ARE YOU SURE THIS"
     410 PRINT"IS WHAT YOU WANT?(Y/N)"
     420 GETC$:IFC$=""THENGOTO420
     430 PRINTP$;"[2 UP]";E$:PRINTE$:PRINTE$
     440 IFC$="N"THENPRINT"[CLR]":GOTO310
     450 IFC$<>"Y"THENGOTO390
     460 L=8:M=2:LR=100:X=1
     470 PRINT"[CLR]":GOTO610
     480 REM "**** READ IN STORED DATA ****"
     490 PRINT"[CLR]"
     500 PRINT;P1$;"INSERT DATA DISK INTO DISC DRIVE..."
     510 PRINT"...THEN PRESS -C- TO CONTINUE"
     520 GETC$:IFC$<>""THEN520
     530 GETC$:IFC$=""THENGOTO530
     540 PRINT"[CLR]";P$;"*** READING DATA FROM DISK ***"
     550 OPEN2,8,2,"DATA,S,R"
     560 INPUT#2,X,L,M,LR,ZI$,YI$,RI$
     570 FORI=1TOX-1
     580 FORJ=1TOL
     590 PRINTP$;TAB(32);"#";I:INPUT#2,D$(I,J):NEXTJ:NEXTI
     600 CLOSE2:PRINT"[CLR]"
     610 REM"**** DATABASE DESIGNMENU ****"
     620 MP=0
     630 PRINT"[CLR]******** YOURDATABASE DESIGN ********"
     640 PRINT"[2 DOWN]1. VIEW/MODIFY INFORMATION"
     650 PRINT"[DOWN]2. ENTER NEW ITEMS"
     660 PRINT"[DOWN]3. VIEW/MODIFY BY ITEM NUMBER "
     690 PRINT"[DOWN]4. END RUN
     790 PRINTP$;E$
     800 PRINTP$;"[DOWN]WHAT WOULD YOU LIKE TO DO?"
     810 GETC$:IFC$<>""THEN810 
     820 GETC$:IFC$=""THEN820
     830 PRINTP$;"[DOWN]";E$
     840 AP=VAL(C$):IFAP>4THEN790
     850 PRINTP$;E$
     860 ONAPGOTO2040,880,1230,2460



                                       34





     870 GOTO630 
     880 REM"**** INSERTNEWITEM****"
     890 IFX>LRTHEN1180
     900 PRINT"[CLR]"
     910 PRINT"[HOME][19 DOWN]";TAB(4-LEN(STR$(X)));X
     930 FORXX=1TOL
     940 PRINT"[HOME][19 DOWN]";TAB(5);"[RVS ON]";XX;"[RVS OFF]"
     950 PRINTP$;E$:PRINTP$;"INPUT LINE";XX;"?"
     960 T$="":INPUTT$
     970 D$(X,XX)=T$:PRINTP$;E$:PRINTE$
     980 IFXX=1ANDT$=""THEN610
